  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 117 PM MDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nUpper ridge builds over the southern Rockies Sat shifting into \nthe central plains by late Sun, as troughing over the sw U.S. \nat least temporarily moves out of the picture. Weekend thus looks\nmostly dry and warmer, with widespread 70s/80s across the area.\nNext upper trough already digging south along the California\nCoast Sunday will begin to bring back moisture to the mountains\nlate Sunday into Sunday night, with at least scattered showers\nalong the Continental Divide from late Sunday afternoon into\nMonday morning. While most models are in agreement of a strong\nupper trough moving through the srn Rockies early next week,  \nmodel/ensemble spread increases significantly starting Mon, with\ntiming/strength/low position issues keeping confidence in \nrainfall timing and amounts rather low at this point. Looking at\n12z data, deterministic runs of both the GFS and ECMWF are  \ndrier for the week than previous solutions, and ensemble QPF has\ndropped somewhat (though there are still a few wet outliers). \nTropical moisture from the remnants of hurricane Polo may come \ninto play as well, though 12z data would suggest bulk of it \nstays south of Colorado. No matter the model solutions, another\nround of meaningful rainfall is expected, and stuck close to \nNBM pops and QPF for now, though won't be surprised if 12z\nmodel trends continue that we'll need to drop precip amounts in\nsubsequent forecasts. In terms of general weather, expect an \nincrease in showers and thunderstorms across the area Mon, then \nfairly widespread rainfall on Tuesday, tapering back to mainly \nshowers on Wednesday. Temperatures will remains slightly above \naverage on Mon, cooling back into the 60s/70s Tue/Wed with \nclouds and precip.",
